#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119464 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "88990b12-e7e0-5e21-9000-45357c07d04d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Equation Governing the Current in an RL Circuit: Constant EMF"}] :question/id #uuid "c7f85c6d-a7d6-5ae6-b82b-7e8608b6411e" :question/math-academy-id "e-21757" :question/problem "Find an equation for the current $I(t)$ (in amperes) in the circuit given in the diagram above, expressing your answer in terms of time $t$ (in seconds) **only**, given that the current when $t = 0\\,\\textrm s$ is $1\\,\\textrm{A}$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1788/Source/Images/d621f8115fb854aa278fd2eea1f93614.png)" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $4\\,\\textrm{V}$, a resistor of resistance of $12\\,\\Omega$, and an inductor of inductance $2\\,\\textrm{H}$.\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RL circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R = 12\\,\\Omega$.\n$V_R = 12I$\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L = 2\\,\\textrm{H}$.\n$V_L = 2\\cdot\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t}$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ in the circuit is\n\n$$\n4 = 12I + 2\\cdot\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 6I = 2\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution $I_c$, we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n$\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I_c}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 6I_c = 0$.\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n$I_c(t) = Ae^{-6t}$,\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0$. So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0$, i.e.,\n$I_p(t) = \\alpha$.\nTo find the value of $\\alpha$, we substitute $I_p = \\alpha$ and $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I_p}{\\mathrm{d}t} = (\\alpha)' = 0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n$0 + 6\\alpha = 2 \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\alpha = \\dfrac13$\nTherefore, the particular solution is $I_p(t) = \\dfrac13$.\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t) = I_c(t) + I_p(t) = Ae^{-6t} + \\dfrac13\n$$\n\nWe're told that the circuit starts with an initial current of $1\\,\\textrm{A}$. So, we can apply the initial condition $I(0) = 1$, and solve for $A{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI(0) &= 1 \\\\\nAe^{-6(0)} + \\frac{1}{3} &= 1 \\\\\nA &= \\frac{2}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $I(t){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI(t) &= \\frac{2}{3}e^{-6t} + \\frac{1}{3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}(2e^{-6t} + 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity $I_c = \\dfrac23e^{-6t}$ is the *transient current*, since it goes to zero as $t \\to \\infty$.\n- The constant $I_p = \\dfrac13$ is the *steady-state current*. As $t\\to\\infty$, the current $I$ approaches the value of the steady-state current."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119465 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "11687da5-3a8e-56cb-adec-e3fa5cadf490" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing an Equation Governing the Current in an RL Circuit"}] :question/id #uuid "9be480e2-68b0-591c-b331-0751a225122c" :question/math-academy-id "e-21758" :question/problem "Find the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ (in amperes) at time $t$ (in seconds) in the circuit given in the diagram above.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1788/Source/Images/8ecd3861795d3513d25a091b8f5aa93b.png)" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of\n\n$$\nE(t) = 7\\sin(3t)\\,\\textrm{V}\n$$\n\na resistor with resistance $14\\,\\Omega$, and an inductor with an inductance of $7\\,\\textrm{H}$.\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RL circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R = 14\\,\\Omega$.\n$V_R = 14I$\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L = 7\\,\\textrm{H}$.\n$V_L = 7\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t}$\n\nThe source voltage (EMF) is given by\n\n$$\nE(t) = 7\\sin(3t)\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ in the circuit is\n\n$$\n7\\sin(3t) = 14I + 7\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n7\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t} = 7\\sin(3t) - 14I\n$$\n\nso\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t} = \\sin(3t) - 2I\n$$\n\nTherefore, the differential equation is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 2I = \\sin(3t)\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119466 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "618f0ac5-e21a-5916-85f1-fbdeee648119" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Equation Governing the Current in an RL Circuit: Sinusoidal EMF"}] :question/id #uuid "2594ae82-9bd8-5ee4-a5a9-4be27b5d9097" :question/math-academy-id "e-21761" :question/problem "Find the amplitude of the steady-state current for the RL circuit shown above, **rounded to two decimal places.**\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1788/Source/Images/596d73053c5f191533d2ca0ece3ff989.png)" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $5\\sin(3t)\\,\\textrm{V}$, a resistor of resistance $5\\,\\Omega$, and an inductor of inductance $1\\,\\textrm{H}$.\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RL circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R = 5\\,\\Omega$.\n$V_R = 5I$\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L = 1\\,\\textrm{H}$.\n$V_L = 1\\cdot\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t} = \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t}$.\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ in the circuit is\n\n$$\n5\\sin(3t) = 5I + \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 5I = 5\\sin(3t)\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution $I_c$, we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n$\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I_c}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 5I_c = 0$.\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n$I_c(t) = Ae^{-5t}$,\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $3t$. So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $3t$, i.e.,\n$I_p(t) = \\alpha\\cos(3t) + \\beta\\sin(3t)$.\nTo find the values of $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$, we substitute\n$I_{p}|= α\\cos (3t) + β\\sin (3t),; \\frac{dI_{p}}{dt}|=-3α\\sin (3t) + 3β\\cos (3t)$,\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n$(-3α\\sin (3t) + 3β\\cos (3t)) + 5(α\\cos (3t) + β\\sin (3t))|= 5\\sin (3t); (-3α + 5β)\\sin (3t) + (5α + 3β)\\cos (3t)|= 5\\sin (3t)$\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n${-3α + 5β = 5\\begin{vmatrix}(equating the coefficients of\\sin (3t)) \\\\ 5α + 3β = 0\\end{vmatrix}(equating the coefficients of\\cos (3t))$\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha = -\\dfrac{15}{34}$, $\\beta = \\dfrac{25}{34}$. Therefore, the particular solution is\n$I_p(t) = -\\dfrac{15}{34}\\cos(3t) + \\dfrac{25}{34}\\sin(3t)$.\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t) = I_c(t) + I_p(t) = Ae^{-5t} - \\dfrac{15}{34}\\cos(3t) + \\dfrac{25}{34}\\sin(3t)\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity $I_c(t) = Ae^{-5t}$ is the *transient current*, since it goes to zero as $t \\to \\infty$.\n- The function $I_p(t) =-\\dfrac{15}{34}\\cos(3t) + \\dfrac{25}{34}\\sin(3t)$ is the *steady-state current*. It governs the current's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to\\infty$, we have\n\n$$\nI(t)\\approx I_p(t) =-\\dfrac{15}{34}\\cos(3t) + \\dfrac{25}{34}\\sin(3t)\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state current is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{\\left(-\\dfrac{15}{34}\\right)^2 + \\left(\\dfrac{25}{34}\\right)^2} \\approx 0.86\\,\\textrm{A}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120191 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "618f0ac5-e21a-5916-85f1-fbdeee648119" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Equation Governing the Current in an RL Circuit: Sinusoidal EMF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "390e5b47-3803-5209-a661-06e32cd98311" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117661}] :question/id #uuid "13747a80-1def-5154-809e-6e4568d44dec" :question/math-academy-id "q-336210" :question/problem "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1788/Source/Images/q-336210.png)\n\nFind the amplitude of the steady-state current for the RL circuit shown above, rounded to two decimal places.The amplitude of the steady-state current is approximately\n{{field-1}} amperes." :question/requires-calculator true}]}
